Consider this C# code:

```csharp
static IEnumerable<int> Codes()
{
    Console.WriteLine("started");
    try
    {
        yield return 6;
        Console.WriteLine("resumed");
        yield return 9;
    }
    finally
    {
        Console.WriteLine("finished");
    }
}

var codes = Codes();
Console.WriteLine("ready");
foreach (var code in codes)
{
    Console.WriteLine(code);
}
```

Explain the output order and how `GetEnumerator()`, `MoveNext()` and `Current` produce it. Then explain how the output and cleanup change if `break;` is added immediately after `Console.WriteLine(code);`.

An iterator contains this body:

```csharp
try
{
    yield return "first";
    yield return "second";
}
finally
{
    Console.WriteLine("released");
}
```

Compare two consumers, each using a fresh enumerator. Both successfully obtain the first item, then throw an exception while processing it. An outer caller catches the exception.

- Consumer A uses `foreach`.
- Consumer B manually calls `GetEnumerator()`, `MoveNext()` and `Current`, but never calls `Dispose()` and has no `using` or cleanup `finally`.

Explain whether `released` is printed in each case, why the outer `catch` matters or does not matter, and what cleanup Consumer B needs.
